content:
maria: Hey — running into weird ESM/CJS errors when bundling the TypeScript SDK for Next edge functions. Error: "SyntaxError: Cannot use import statement outside a module" at runtime. Using Next 13 edge runtime with a small serverless handler. Anyone seen this?

jonathan: Which bundler target are you using? Vercel edge needs ESM-only for edge runtime. Are you `require()`ing the SDK anywhere?

maria: No `require()` — all `import`. package.json has "type": "module". Build uses Next's default SWC. But local vite builds (for functions) bundle differently and when deployed it fails.

kevin: Common culprits: transitive dependency compiled to CJS, or a package.json `main` pointing to CJS entry. Try forcing the ESM entry with `import pkg from 'redwood-sdk/esm'` (if exposed) or aliasing in the bundler.

leo: For Vite, you can add resolve.alias: { 'redwood-sdk': '/node_modules/redwood-sdk/dist/esm/index.js' } — ugly but works as a quick check. Also check package exports field.

maria: Ah okay. Another issue: auth code uses `crypto.createHmac` (node builtin) — that breaks on edge where `crypto` isn't available. Any patterns?

jonathan: Use Web Crypto APIs on edge. Example:
```js
// HMAC-SHA256 using SubtleCrypto in edge runtime
async function hmac(key, msg) {
  const k = await crypto.subtle.importKey('raw', new TextEncoder().encode(key), { name: 'HMAC', hash: 'SHA-256' }, false, ['sign']);
  const sig = await crypto.subtle.sign('HMAC', k, new TextEncoder().encode(msg));
  return btoa(String.fromCharCode(...new Uint8Array(sig)));
}
```
kevin: Also note: many bundlers polyfill `crypto` for Node, which masks the problem locally. Deploy will still fail. Better to keep SDK auth abstraction that uses `globalThis.crypto` when available.

sarah: Quick check for streaming: In an edge handler I'm trying to stream tokens back using Response and ReadableStream. Works locally but Vercel edge says "Response.body is null". Any tips?

leo: Vercel's edge currently has some streaming limitations. If you need streaming in production, confirm provider supports it. Otherwise fallback to chunked JSON with small buffers. Also ensure you don't call `res.json()` before returning a stream.

maria: For retries: implemented simple fetch wrapper with exponential backoff, but AbortController behaves slightly differently on edge. Any recommended patterns?

jonathan: Use async retry that clones the request body for non-idempotent methods or avoid retrying POST with bodies unless you can safely replay. For AbortController, pass the same signal to fetch and cancel timers on retry. Example pattern: 
```js
async function fetchWithRetry(input, init = {}, retries = 3) {
  for (let i = 0; i < retries; i++) {
    const ac = new AbortController();
    const timer = setTimeout(() => ac.abort(), 10_000);
    try {
      const res = await fetch(input, {...init, signal: ac.signal});
      clearTimeout(timer);
      if (res.ok) return res;
      if (res.status < 500) return res; // don't retry 4xx
    } catch (e) {
      if (e.name === 'AbortError') throw e;
      if (i === retries - 1) throw e;
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 2 ** i * 200));
    } finally { clearTimeout(timer); }
  }
}
```
kevin: FYI for Next edge, add `runtime: 'experimental-edge'`? (depends on Next version). Check host docs.

maria: Thanks — helpful. Anyone willing to review a tiny PR that adds an ESM-only entry + edge-friendly crypto fallback and docs for bundlers?

jonathan: I can take a look this afternoon. Ping me in PR description with repro steps.

leo: I'll add a small note in dev docs about polyfills and Vite aliasing.

devex-bot: Reminder — please add a short changelog entry if you change auth behavior (security review). :white_check_mark:

maria: Will do. Thanks all!
